</td>
                                            <td class="subtasks">                </td>
                                            <td class="issuelinks">                </td>
                                            <td class="environment">      generic
  </td>
                                            <td class="description">      For development of an avro-based transport for remote OSGi services I had to wrap &amp; unwind transport-level exceptions into proper exceptions thrown to a client. One such scenario is accessing a disconnected/dead endpoint, which should be a detectable condition. This works fine with the SaslSocketTransceiver (where my wrapper can properly detect the IO Exception), but not with Netty which hiccups with an internal NPE.<br/>
Generally speaking any transport-level and remote-endpoint exceptions must not only be clearly detectable (either directly by client code or wrapper infrastructure around avro) but also distinguishable from another.<br/>

  </td>
